From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: blurbery <271863866+blurbery@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vo_avfoundation: frame path, colour tags and composites

The frame path of the AVFoundation video output.

Software frames (NV12, yuv420p, P010 and 10-bit and 12-bit planar 4:2:0)
are copied into 420v or x420 buffers and tagged from the image's own
parameters: primaries, transfer function, matrix and a colour space made
from them. VideoToolbox buffers keep the decoder's tags. Every buffer gets
the pixel aspect from the configured parameters, or loses a stale one.
Dolby Vision passthrough frames (those carrying VideoToolbox's
DolbyVisionRPUData attachment) that lack a transfer function get it from
the image's parameters, so profile 5 is presented as PQ. Samples use a
cached format description and nanosecond presentation times.

For Picture in Picture the subtitles are composited into the picture.
Biplanar 4:2:0 frames get the integer YUV blend in place. Before blending
into a decoder buffer, the rows the blend will change are kept. That buffer
may be on screen, so nothing writes to it until it is presented again:
without a cue its rows are written back first, and with one (a redraw) the
final rows are prepared elsewhere from the kept original pixels and each
changed row is written once, those gaining the new cue before those that
only lose the old one. An unchanged cue writes nothing, and the picture
never passes through a state without a cue. Switching compositing off
touches no buffer, and kept rows for a buffer that is not presented again
are let go unwritten. Unlike the engine Vivid ships today, every presented
image therefore carries its current subtitle exactly once, with no stale
cue under a new one and no burned-in cue after compositing ends, while the
decoder's buffer keeps exactly its own attachments. No second video buffer
is made, and only the last composited buffer is held. Other formats, and a
blend that produces nothing, go through Core Image, which renders to
half-float RGBA for 10-bit 4:2:0 sources and to BGRA for everything else;
a frame Core Image cannot read goes out without the subtitle. A composite
that would change the frame's size is discarded.

Applies to mpv v0.41.0 with Vivid's earlier patches.

